    rationale:
      "Dispatched a read-only research agent (general-purpose) briefed on AGENT_PROTOCOL's full text and the current tail of ITERATIONS/BUSINESS_DECISIONS (the alert()-to-inline-aria series, the Buffer CTA-label fix, and the partially-fixed leads-client.tsx stale-prop-resync bug, of which 8 of 9 useState(initialX) arrays remain an explicitly documented follow-up), asked to independently verify by reading actual source rather than trusting the log's own \"clean\" claims. It found that every other client-facing send action with a durable sent-marker (marketing/voice pack email, bundle buyer resend, checkout recovery, Starter upsell, Stripe Connect onboarding link) has a concurrency guard against a second dashboard tab or a double-click sending a real duplicate email — but `replyToInquiryAction` (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts), the CRM reply-to-visitor flow, did not, despite being the one send in this whole family that reaches an actual end customer rather than the operator's own paid client. Independently re-verified by reading `recordReply` (convex/inquiries.ts) and `sqliteRecordClientInquiryReply` (src/lib/db/sqlite-queries.ts) directly: both gated only on `existing === null || status === \"archived\"` (the already-shipped inquiry-reply-archive-race-guard), with no counter/CAS check — and `inquiryCanReceiveReply` (src/lib/inquiries/reply.ts) deliberately allows replying to an already-replied inquiry (a legitimate follow-up is a real feature), so there was no \"already replied\" short-circuit anywhere in the stack. Failure scenario: two open dashboard tabs on the same inquiry's reply composer (or a double-click before the button's `isPending`-disabled state applies, since `isPending` is per-tab local React state) both call `replyToInquiryAction` with the same stale read — both pass `inquiryCanReceiveReply`, both persist via `recordClientInquiryReply` (no count check to reject the second), and `sendInquiryReply` fires twice, both silently reporting `success: true` while the real visitor receives a duplicate reply email.\n\nFix mirrors `recordStarterUpsellSent`'s plain expected-count compare-and-swap (not `markMarketingPackSentToClient`'s one-shot \"already sent\" gate, since a later, deliberate follow-up reply must still succeed against a fresh read): `ClientInquiry` (src/lib/inquiries/types.ts), the Convex `clientInquiries` schema, and the SQLite `client_inquiries` table (via the existing `addLeadCol`-style migration pattern in sqlite-schema.ts, mirroring how `operatorReply`/`repliedAt` were added) all gained `replySentCount`. `recordReply` (convex/inquiries.ts) and `sqliteRecordClientInquiryReply` (src/lib/db/sqlite-queries.ts) now take `expectedReplySentCount` and reject (return null) when the row's current count no longer matches, otherwise advancing it by one alongside the existing status/operatorReply/repliedAt patch — exactly the same return-null signal the archive-race guard already used, so both guards compose without a third return shape. `recordClientInquiryReply` (src/lib/db.ts) threads the new parameter through both the Convex and SQLite-fallback branches. Because a `null` result from the mutation is now ambiguous (missing row, archived race, or duplicate-send race), `replyToInquiryAction` re-fetches the inquiry via the already-imported `getClientInquiryById` on a `null` result and branches to report the accurate current reason instead of one hardcoded message covering three different causes — the same re-fetch-and-branch approach `commerce-order-paid-out-race-message-accuracy` used for an analogous ambiguous-failure case. No Convex API map change was needed (recordReply is `mutation`-registered already; only its args/handler changed) and no replace()-based Convex mutation conditional-spread field list needed updating (every `clientInquiries` mutation in convex/inquiries.ts uses `ctx.db.patch`, not `.replace()`, so adding a field required no cross-mutation audit).\n\nShipped test coverage across all four layers: convex/inquiries.test.ts (a new race-guard test proving a stale expected count is rejected while a fresh-count follow-up succeeds, plus updated existing recordReply calls to pass the now-required expectedReplySentCount), src/lib/db/sqlite-queries.test.ts (mirrored SQLite-level race-guard test), src/lib/db.test.ts (mirrored db.ts-orchestration-level test across the Convex/SQLite boundary), and src/app/dashboard/leads/reply-to-inquiry-action.test.ts (split the old single \"surfaces not-found/archived-race when the mark returns null\" test into three: not-found, archived-race, and the new duplicate-send-race message, each driving the action-level re-fetch-and-branch through a distinct getClientInquiryById second-call mock). Verified the SQLite-level test actually catches a regression: temporarily disabled the `expectedReplySentCount` CAS check in sqliteRecordClientInquiryReply, confirmed the new duplicate-rejection assertion failed with the duplicate write actually landing (`operatorReply: \"Duplicate reply\"` visible in the failure diff), then restored the fix — a stray `git checkout --` while restoring briefly wiped the whole file back to HEAD, caught immediately via `git diff --stat` showing zero changes, and the fix was re-applied from scratch and re-verified rather than blind-trusted.\n\nVerified end-to-end: npm ci (unchanged) + npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run full suite (3465 passed, 210 files — 5 net new: 1 in db.test.ts, 1 in sqlite-queries.test.ts, 1 in convex/inquiries.test.ts, 2 in reply-to-inquiry-action.test.ts from the 1-test-split-into-3) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings) + npm run dev smoke check curling /dashboard/leads (200, \"All leads\" heading present, no application-error/digest markers) and /docs/process (200). Killed the dev server and removed the stray gitignored local.db it created before this commit; confirmed git status showed only the intended source files changed. src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx gained a new paragraph directly before the closing 'Tech stack' section naming the gap, the reused CAS pattern, and the re-fetch-and-branch message-accuracy fix. No NEXT_STEPS entry (a bug fix on an already-shipped operator CRM surface, not a new capability) — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Explicitly left as a follow-up for a future session, per the research agent's own runner-up note: the leads-client.tsx stale-prop-resync series still has 8 of 9 useState(initialX) arrays unprotected (only `leads` itself was fixed in leads-client-stale-prop-resync) — each is a small, independently-verifiable change per the prior session's own scoping. Diff-gate anchor moves to 6655bb3 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#872).",
