    rationale:
      "The agent reported, after extensive investigation across CAS-guard Convex/SQLite symmetry, ctx.db.replace() field-completeness, dashboard/digest wiring, rate-limiting, HTML/JSON-LD escaping, and money-separation invariants, that it could not find a bug with real business/money/security impact — consistent with this codebase's now well-documented hardening. Its least-bad candidate, independently re-verified before implementing: `addLeadAction` (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts) — the manual Add-lead Server Action — was the one lead-creation path that never validated `category` against the same allowlist CSV import has always enforced. `csv-import.ts`'s `parseCategory` hard-rejects an unrecognized value with a clear, row-level operator error (`Invalid category \"X\" (use restaurant|salon|...)`); `normalizeLeadCreateFields` (src/lib/leads/create-fields.ts), the shared field-cap normalizer both create paths funnel through, only ever used `category` to pick a `defaultTagline` fallback, with no allowlist check anywhere on the manual path. Confirmed directly: a Server Action's declared parameter type (`BusinessCategory`) is not enforced at runtime against an arbitrary caller — the dashboard's own `<select>` only ever submits a valid value, but a raw POST (a scripted call, or a malformed client) did not have to go through it. Traced the actual failure mode: `upsertLead` (src/lib/db.ts) is Convex-primary via `LEAD_UPSERT_CONVEX_PRIMARY` (`requireConvex: true`), and Convex's own `categoryValidator` (a `v.union` of literals, convex/leads.ts) does reject an invalid category — but only by throwing an unstructured `ArgumentValidationError` that `addLeadAction`'s outer catch turns into a raw, un-operator-friendly message, unlike CSV import's clean rejection. Worse, when Convex is not configured at all (`NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL` unset, the common local/demo-mode path), `upsertLead` calls `sqliteUpsertLead` directly with zero validation, silently persisting an arbitrary category string. This never actually crashes the public preview page — both backends' read paths (`mapConvexLeadRow`/`mapSqliteRowToLead`) already run `normalizeLeadCategory`, which safely falls back to `\"generic\"` for any unrecognized stored value (see `leads-category-tier-read-normalize-parity`) — but it silently coerces a typo'd or malformed category to `\"generic\"` on every later read with zero feedback to the operator, a data-quality/UX inconsistency between the two create paths rather than a live crash.\n\nWhile confirming the gap, also found `csv-import.ts` maintained its own hand-written `CATEGORIES` Set (a literal duplicate of `restaurant`/`salon`/.../`generic`) rather than importing the canonical `BUSINESS_CATEGORIES` array already exported by `category-normalize.ts` — the exact drift-risk pattern (three separate lists to update by hand any time a category is added or removed: this Set, Convex's `categoryValidator`, and `category-normalize.ts`'s own array) that plausibly let this validation gap on the Add-lead path go unnoticed through the recent ten-vertical BusinessCategory series, each of which had to remember to touch csv-import.ts's Set and its hardcoded error-message string by hand.\n\nShipped: src/lib/leads/create-fields.ts — `normalizeLeadCreateFields` now hard-rejects a `category` not present in `BUSINESS_CATEGORIES` (imported from `category-normalize.ts`), returning `{ ok: false, error: 'Invalid category \"X\" (use restaurant|salon|...)' }` in the same style CSV import already uses, checked right after the existing `businessName` validation and before any field is soft-capped. src/lib/leads/csv-import.ts — deduped: `CATEGORIES` is now `new Set<BusinessCategory>(BUSINESS_CATEGORIES)` instead of a hand-written literal list, and the row-level error message now builds its allowed-values list from `BUSINESS_CATEGORIES.join(\"|\")` instead of a separately hardcoded pipe-separated string — behavior and wording are unchanged (verified byte-identical against the existing csv-import.test.ts assertion of the exact message text), but a future category addition now only needs to update `category-normalize.ts`'s array for this pair of checks, not three separate places (Convex's `categoryValidator` stays its own differently-shaped `v.union`/`v.literal` list by necessity — Convex validators can't be built from a plain string array). src/lib/leads/create-fields.test.ts — two new cases: an unrecognized category is rejected with the exact CSV-parity error message, and every one of the ten canonical categories is accepted. src/app/dashboard/leads/add-lead-action.test.ts — one new action-level case proving the rejection surfaces through addLeadAction as `success: false` before any `getLeadBySlug`/`upsertLead` call, mirroring the existing over-long-businessName test's shape. Verified the new tests actually catch the regression: temporarily short-circuited the new check to `if (false && ...)`, confirmed both the new create-fields.test.ts case and the new add-lead-action.test.ts case failed (the action returned `success: true` for a nonsense category instead of rejecting it), then restored the fix and reran to confirm both pass. src/app/docs/architecture/page.tsx — new paragraph directly before the closing 'Tech stack' section naming the gap, why it doesn't crash (read-time normalize already covers that), the fix, and the CATEGORIES-Set dedup. No NEXT_STEPS change — this is a real, previously-unaddressed data-integrity/UX-parity bug fix in an already-shipped capability, not any of the five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable), which remain operator/legal/infra-blocked and unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 94a2baf (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#874).\n\nVerified end-to-end: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (create-fields.test.ts, csv-import.test.ts, add-lead-action.test.ts — all passed) then full suite (3469 passed, 210 files — 3 net new).",
